    {
      "id": "71608a0ea153eb3c85006f542e61a5633c33434d",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": true,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 978451636,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> Also `ENC_UTF_16` would be UTF-16 in host byte order, or with a BOM,\n\nWhat would \"UTF-16 in whatever byte order is specified for all multi-byte numbers in this packet\" be?  See epan/dissectors/packet-dcerpc.c.  That *could* be done with, for example\n\n    (DREP_ENC_INTEGER(drep) == ENC_LITTLE_ENDIAN ? ENC_UTF_16LE : ENC_UTF_16BE)\n\nbut that isn't as simple as\n\n    ENC_UTF_16|DREP_ENC_INTEGER(drep)\n\nwhich is how it's handled now.\n\n> `ENC_UTF_16LE` and `ENC_UTF_16BE` are well-known names coming from the standard.\n\n    #define ENC_UTF_16LE (ENC_UTF_16|ENC_LITTLE_ENDIAN)\n    #define ENC_UTF_16BE (ENC_UTF_16|ENC_BIG_ENDIAN)\n\ngives you the names *without* having to modify the processing of the encoding; it also permits\n\n    ENC_UTF_16|DREP_ENC_INTEGER(drep)\n\n\"UTF-16 with BOM\", if required, would be `ENC_UTF_16_WITH_BOM` or something such as that.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2022-06-09T04:25:36.347Z",
          "updated_at": "2022-06-09T06:52:08.203Z",
          "system": false,
